A||||14||Ken Wittlief||my rear view mirror fell off the windsheild!||||11||
Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||07-31-2001||10:11 AM||||went to Cedar Point this last weekend (thurs - sat) and took the saturn so I could tow the Coleman poptart camper, left the Fiero on the lawn<P>  and monday morning I find the rearview mirror laying on the floor by the gas pedal!<P>  Anyone ever have this happen to them?!<P>  I think my windsheild was replaced once - it has the blue tint across the top - which I dont think is stock<P>  Im doing a 24hr JB Weld epoxy attempt on it right now, will see if it holds - but its been my experience in the past that once a mirror comes off the glass, getting it to bond again can be difficult [to put it mildly].<P>  but it really blows my mind that the mirror would simply fall off while the car was parked on the lawn - I could see it coming loose while trying to adjust it - but this is weird!||||reg||11
Z||000001||mbramble||07-31-2001||10:33 AM||||Same thing happened in my pickup truck last year.  The windshield had been replaced a couple of years before.<P>You can by rear view mirror glue at AutoZone, etc.  Specially made just for the job and only takes a few seconds to bond.||||reg||1
Z||000002||isthiswhereiputausername?||07-31-2001||10:37 AM||||try a hammer and a few nails?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <P>J/K   just stop by any windshield shop, they will reglue it for next to nothing..<P>I had it done last year to my wifes car, cost $2.00 <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Steve Normington||07-31-2001||10:37 AM||||I've found mirrors on the floor in the morning. My guess is that the expansion/contraction of the glass/metal button is what causes them to fall off. If you use the stuff specifically designed for rear-view mirror buttons, you shouldn't have a problem.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tigger||07-31-2001||10:57 AM||||Have you tried a staple gun?  If all fails, use the handyman secret weapon... duck tape!  j/k   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>One time I pulled a holeshot with my 2m4 and the rearview mirror went flying back at me.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||deceler8||07-31-2001||12:06 PM||||I've never had much success with the rear view mirror adhesive. But I've had great results using clear silicone to reattach the button to the glass.<P>The advantage of silicone is that you don't have to hold the button in place while it cures...the silicone is sticky enough to hold the button until it cures.<P>And since it won't be in your field of vision, probably any silicone (including the form-a-gasket RTV stuff) will work equally well.<P>The button on one of my cars fell off 8 years ago and the silicone is still holding it. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||07-31-2001||12:37 PM||||I've used the $2.00 kits from WalMart many times with great results.<P>Just follow the instructions exactly.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||07-31-2001||02:42 PM||||ken, go to a glass shop like Safelite. Theyll install it for you with better than OEM adhesive. The store bought stuff never holds for long, its just superglue. Ive had it redone in 3 or 4 cars and they never even charged me for it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||84Bill||07-31-2001||02:44 PM||||Yup happend to me a few times, get the glue and goup it into place.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||07-31-2001||05:08 PM||||Happened to me, too.<BR>Pep Boys  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> sells a kit for re-attaching the mirror. Made by Permatex. Worked okay for me. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Vader||07-31-2001||09:45 PM||||Summer is here, and the sun is shining brightly.  The thermal expansion rates of the materials, along with the softened adhesive, can dump a lot of glass-mounted mirrors into the cup holders these days.  It's as common as overheating.<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Let the bodies hit the floor!</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000011||maryjane||07-31-2001||09:46 PM||||Yep I've used the Permatex kind and a couple others, including one with an activator. None work for too long. The glass shops seem to do the best job and don't cost much more than the stuff you buy at auto parts places. Mine only falls off during summer months, so I guess the reply about heat and contraction is accurate.||||reg||1
Z||000012||MrPBody||07-31-2001||11:18 PM||||Ditch the old mirror; install a 5-panel Wink.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ken Wittlief||08-01-2001||10:45 AM||||The JB Weld is holding it own - mirror is back on. Will let you guys know if it fails, but for now its looking solid.||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||08-01-2001||07:26 PM||||The best repair kits I've used are the ones that come with new plastic buttons and glue. I've never seen one of those come back off.<P>The Permatex kits will work well most of the time. You have to make sure the glass is absolutly clean. Laquer thinner followed by 90% isopropyl alcohol will do a good job. Between the two they remove any dirt and residue from other cleaning products that get used in cars. Also with these you want to take some fine sand paper to the button to get rid of glue residue and oxide on the metal.<P>Will be interesting to see how JB weld holds up. That area can get really hot. ||||reg||1
